[{:seon.task/name "avet-seek-allocates"
  :seon.task/plan "`seon.db/eid` is one `(d/datoms db :avet a v)` seek, the path of ``datahike.db.utils/entid``. It cost 13-17 us and 23-32 KB at 100-10k tasks, growing with log n, against ~3 us and ~1.3 KB of work. Fixed in part: Datahike now takes a unique attribute's value in one descent with no slice, so `seon.task-cost-test/finding-a-task-by-its-id-is-one-index-seek` measures 2,384 B and 5.2 us at 1,000 tasks (was 8,832 B), and its `:overrun` waiver no longer names this task but `jolt.backend/a-deftype-method-call-takes-a-site-cache` (Jolt's constructing the bound datom, the descent's method calls, d/datoms' multimethod and varargs, protocol dispatch). What is left of this task: `seon.program-cost-test`'s record-tx proof still carries `:overrun {:task \"seon.db/avet-seek-allocates\" ...}` (its measure of 2026-11-10: 90 KB and 20 calls at 300 forms; whether it was taken before or after the seek fix is not stated), and `seon.doc-cost-test`'s comment cites ~17 us and ~33 KB a seek. Correct: re-measure those two with `seon.cost/measure` on the merged seek fix, move the waiver in program_cost_test to the task that now owns what remains (or delete it if green), correct the doc-cost-test numbers, and then remove this task."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.task-cost-test/finding-a-task-by-its-id-is-one-index-seek}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-small-transaction-costs-its-datoms"
  :seon.task/plan "`d/with` of one 5-datom definition record costs 221-233 us and 148-162 KB, flat in program size (100-10k vars). The work is ~15 index inserts at ~1.3 us (`seon.cost/units` :sorted/assoc), 20-30 us: ~10x over. The cause is Datahike's per-transaction overhead on Jolt (tx report, schema checks, index wrappers): find it with seon.profile and fix it in our fork. Correct: the cost proof green at its estimate. The named proof is not written. Entry, measured 2026-11-06: `(seon.program/record-tx db {:id \"app.z/(defn z [x] x)\" :src \"(defn z [x] x)\" :ns 'app.z :ns-form? false :defs [{:var 'app.z/z :kind :fn :contract \"[:=> [:cat :int] :int]\"}] :declares #{} :methods [] :impls [] :refs #{} :requires #{} :banned #{} :inline-defs #{}})` on `(my.test/world)` is 4 ops and `d/with` of them writes 11 datoms, which `seon.cost/measure` reads at 343 us and 385 KB."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.task-cost-test/a-definition-record-costs-its-datoms}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-schema-transaction-costs-its-attributes"
  :seon.task/plan "The one schema transaction (`seon.db/schema`) costs 27.1 ms and 31.9 MB in memory on 2026-11-06 (`seon.cost/measure` of `(datahike.core/db-with (datahike.core/empty-db {} {:keep-history? false :schema-flexibility :write}) seon.db/schema)`: 408 attributes, 1,286 datoms; the first half 12.8 ms and 15.3 MB, so linear), against 0.12 ms for a 2-datom note. When first measured it was 45-49 ms for 129 attributes and 407 datoms (half: 15.5-16.7 ms, superlinear), because `update-rschema` recomputed the whole rschema after every schema datom. That is fixed in our Datahike (1d96b971, 2026-10-28, in the pin 594b331f): `update-rschema` (reference-code/datahike src/datahike/db/transaction.cljc:192) updates the reverse schema for the one attribute that changed (`dbu/rschema-update`). What is left is the constant: about 21 us a datom against ~1.3 us for an index insert (`seon.cost/units` :sorted/assoc); its cause is not found. Estimate: ~1,286 datom inserts at ~1.3 us, about 2 ms. The named proof is not written. The schema transaction runs once per store (a check run, the test store), not per boot: it prices every store's creation and any schema change."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-cost-test/a-schema-transaction-is-linear-in-its-attributes}}
 {:seon.task/name "two-processes-branch-one-store-at-once"
  :seon.task/plan "Since task each-id-is-issued-once (worktree session global-ids, 2026-10-23) entity ids and transaction ids come from one page every process shares (seon.db/ids!), reseeded from the heads at open: of what follows, only the roster append remains. Several processes writing different branches of one store is safe and is the design: each commits its own branch (content-addressed immutable node keys plus its one head key, each put one LMDB write transaction; konserve.jolt.lmdb:86). Changing the roster is not: `d/branch!` (``datahike.versioning/branch!``) appends the :branches log with ``konserve.core/append`` (reference-code/konserve src/konserve/core.cljc:570), which reads the log head and then makes three writes (the element, the old last element's :next, the head) under go-locked, a lock of this process only (konserve.jolt.lmdb's blob lock is a no-op token, lmdb.jolt:28). Two processes branching at once can each link after the same last element, and one branch drops out of `d/branches` (its head key stays, so `seon.db/branch?` still finds it); a process SIGKILLed between the writes leaves the same tear. Production has one root per store, the only process that branches (the writer's :spawn and :clone-branch); several roots on one store (parallel check steps as separate roots, a standing bin/root beside a T5 run on <SEON_RUNS>/store) meet both. Correct: either the roster append is one LMDB write transaction (a konserve.jolt.lmdb -update-in over the three keys in one txn, on a jolt/<topic> branch of our konserve), or one root per store at a time is a failure point with its edge (`seon.db/open!` refusing a store another live root holds, e.g. by an flock on <store>/root.lock). Parallel checks share one root, each step on its own branch (production's shape), with the crash steps (durable, window, resume, orphan) run with nothing else on the store. The named proof exists and is green on 2026-11-06 (`bin/test seon.db-test/two-processes-branch-one-store`, SHARD line read: two processes make 50 branches each, every branch is listed), so it does not fail on the tear described here. docs/storage.md#collection says production runs one root per store and gives `:seon.config.store/sole-writer?`."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/two-processes-branch-one-store}}
 {:seon.task/name "dead-readers-are-cleared-at-open"
  :seon.task/plan "LMDB reuses a freed page only when no reader's snapshot is older than the txn that freed it, and a SIGKILLed process's reader slot pins its snapshot until mdb_reader_check clears it; nothing in src calls ``konserve.jolt.lmdb-ffi/reader-check``. Reproduction (jolt/runner e0efb69): four check steps at once, each its own root and agents on one LMDB store, filled the map: mdb_stat showed 65,534 of 65,536 pages used, 60,055 of them free (live data ~85 MB). Correct: clear dead readers where a process opens the env (root boot, agent open: one call), and keep read txns short. The map's size is `:seon.config.store/lmdb-map-bytes` (64 GiB, its provenance stated in resources/seon/config/default.edn). Checked 2026-11-06: ``konserve.jolt.lmdb-ffi/reader-check`` exists (lmdb_ffi.jolt:219) and nothing in src calls it; the named proof is not written."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-dead-readers-slot-does-not-pin-freed-pages}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-collection-runs-beside-live-agents"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: root collects the store only at quiescence with no agent live: `seon.reconcile/quiet` asks once per quiet spell, root's api refuses :collect while an agent is live, and the writer runs `seon.db/collect!` (docs/storage.md#collection). A commit can reuse a value out of line that a concurrent sweep would take, so on a busy root garbage from deleted branches waits for a quiet spell. Correct (the design in docs/storage.md#collection): the mark runs outside LMDB's writer lock; a short delta mark and the sweep run inside one write transaction; a commit that reuses a value re-asserts it in its own transaction. Deletes the api's no-agent-live reason of :collect (seon.root, the :write clause refusing :collect while `(:agents st)` is non-empty). The named proof is not written; docs/storage.md#collection says collecting beside live agents is not built."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-collection-beside-a-committing-agent-keeps-every-value-it-reuses}}
 {:seon.task/name "values-compress-with-a-trained-dictionary"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: every stored value is compressed by the codec `:seon.config.store/compressor` names (zstd level 3, one frame per value, `konserve.jolt.zstd`), each value's header naming its codec (docs/storage.md#compression). Measured there: a dictionary trained on our own values compresses small ones 2-5x better, and faster; it is unused because it needs a corpus that represents the running system. Correct: a dictionary trained (zstd's ZDICT_trainFromBuffer) on a kept store's values, stored in the store under its digest; a value's header names the dictionary, so a store holding values written with and without one reads both; retraining adds a dictionary and never rewrites old values. A konserve fork change on a jolt/<topic> branch, root's go first. Measure bytes per per-turn commit first. Neither named proof is written."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-value-written-with-a-dictionary-reads-back seon.db-test/a-dictionary-shrinks-what-a-commit-writes}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-one-eval-commit-costs-what-it-writes"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-10-24 on jolt/agent-conversation (an agent commits after each eval): inside an agent, one model eval of (+ 20 21) with its record costs 137-151k ticks and 0.48-0.54 MB; the seon.agent/commit! after it (Datahike's commit-pending! of that one eval's datoms) 440-449k ticks and 3.19-3.24 MB, the first after the fork 7.06M ticks and 33.3 MB (seon.profile/measure!, three rounds). A commit of a few hundred bytes of datoms allocates about 3 MB: too much for what it writes, though about a millisecond against a model call's 16-18 s (root kept D, 2026-10-24). Cause to find: where a one-eval commit allocates (the tail-leaf copy-on-write this file's store task measured, values inline in the index leaves twice, the commit record and its graph record, the konserve write path, the LZ4/zstd node codec, or the agent's run-ahead apply), by allocation per function (seon.profile/touched) on one commit; then the fix at that owner, in the fork if it is Datahike's. Proof: a cost proof that a commit costs what it writes (bytes allocated within a bound of the datoms' and the rewritten path's size, linear in the datoms, flat in the branch's size), beside seon.db-test/a-commit-costs-what-it-writes, which measures bytes written. The named proof is not written. Task a-commit-of-a-few-datoms-writes-what-it-changed (2026-11-01) split where a commit allocates, per function, and names the same proof (a cost proof of one commit's bytes in seon.db)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-commit-allocates-what-it-writes}}
 {:seon.task/name "opening-a-store-costs-what-it-opens"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured again 2026-11-11 at 1, 10, 100 and 1,000 branches: `seon.db/open!` costs 0.55 M, 0.76 M, 2.9 M and 24.2 M ticks (70.9 MB, 2,017 konserve reads at 1,000): 23.7k ticks, 68 KB and 2 reads a branch in the store, all of it `seon.db/ids!` (Datahike's `set-ids!`, `roster-max`: the roster log folded and every branch's head read); Datahike's connect is flat at 4 reads; ``test-runner/program-db`` adds a read a roster entry through ``d/branches``. Being built (the forks' session, branch not yet landed): the ids page carries a stamp of the OS boot identity and its own file's device and inode; `ids!` trusts a page whose stamp matches (a process crash loses nothing: the page is a shared mapping) and reads the heads only for a new, copied or post-reboot page, then stamps; expected 0.35 M ticks and 1.7 MB at any branch count. Its proofs are kills: a SIGKILLed writer, a cloned store with and without its ids file, a rewritten boot stamp, a cost proof flat in branches. Earlier text follows. Found 2026-10-27 by the per-function counters (seon.profile/touched, task seon.proofs/tests-are-facts) when seon.proofs-test/a-kept-tree-costs-its-lookup-not-its-program went red at 1.25x its byte estimate: one test-runner/program-db call of a kept tree is 21 contracted calls, 1.49M ticks and 5.57 MB at 10 functions, 1.59M and 5.91 MB at 1,000. Of its bytes, `seon.db/ids!` 1.32 MB, growing to 1.62 MB as the process's program store gains branches (it moves the ids page past every branch head's ids: datahike.writing/set-ids!), so the proof's bytes depend on the branches earlier tests in the shard kept, not on its own input; `seon.db/install-missing!` 1.35 MB a call though nothing is missing (it compares every attribute's definition with the store's); `seon.fx/git-run` 0.93 MB over 5 calls; `seon.db/open-main!` 0.69 MB. Correct: ids! reads the heads' ids once a store is opened, not per branch walked again, or costs the branches changed since; install-missing! costs nothing when the store's schema digest is the program's (one value compared). Proof: the cost proof green with its estimate unchanged, and its bytes the same with 1 and 100 kept branches. Fixed 2026-10-28 (jolt/test-audit): install-missing! reads the db's own (:schema db) rather than d/schema, which rebuilt the schema map on every open; it leaves the top six allocators, and one program-db call is 4.65/4.98/5.34 MB at 10/100/1,000 functions (was 5.60/5.93/6.26 MB in full run 3), 1.07x the estimate. Still open, measured 2026-10-31 from seon.proofs-test/a-process-plans-on-more-scratch-stores-than-its-undo-entries's recorded per-function costs (317 M ticks for 12 fresh stores of a two-file program): seon.db/seed! of a fresh store is 259 M of them, all in install-missing! (24 calls, 260 M self: ~11 M ticks a call), against 4 M for import-tx and 2 M for the reach graph; a fresh store's whole schema installed in two transactions costs ~22 M ticks, which every test with its own store and every check seed pays (task seon.db/a-store-is-made-whole-or-not-at-all's create, ~22 M, is the same work). Correct: a fresh store costs what a store copy does (seed one, clone it), or the install costs the attributes, not ~11 M a transaction at 1,000 measured alone. It still compares every attribute (no rebuild); the one digest compare is not done. Left: ids! is 1.32/1.62/1.92 MB, about 0.3 MB for every branch the store holds, because datahike.writing/set-ids! reads each branch head's whole record (roster-max) for two numbers. Fixed in Datahike 148ea9a9 (jolt/set-ids-reads-the-heads-not-their-records, pinned on jolt/test-audit): a compact head carries its commit's max-eid and max-tx, so ids! reads each head alone; one program-db call is now 4.19/4.28/4.38 MB at 10/100/1,000 functions and ids! 0.85/0.92/0.98 MB (was 1.32/1.62/1.92). Left: install-missing!'s one digest compare in place of every attribute's."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-roots-heap-costs-what-it-reads"
  :seon.task/plan "Every agent inherits the fork server's heap, so an agent's start heap is root's live heap: 0.97 GB on a fresh store and 1.32 GB on the standing one (355k datoms against 178k; seon.agent at agent.clj caps it at that plus :seon.config.agent/heap-extra-bytes). The 0.36 GB grows with every recorded test run (106k :seon.proof* datoms) and every write (48k seon.tx and db datoms), with no bound. Measured 2026-10-28 on an APFS clone of runs/store, at main d96168afb1:
(1) The node cache is not the cause. :seon.config.store/node-cache-bytes bounds it, but by count: datahike.index.node-cache weighs every node at 4,096 bytes, so the bound is 32,768 nodes. A just-booted standing root fills it (25k misses in 20 s; 12k on a fresh store, so boot reads in proportion to the store, not to what it needs). Evicting all 32,768 nodes and collecting the maximum generation freed 50 MB (1.419 -> 1.367 GB live).
(2) Realized values held outside the cache are the cause. Chez object-counts after a full collection, standing against fresh: vectors 101 MB (1.80 M) against 29 MB (352k), strings 183 MB against 104 MB, pairs 82 MB against 63 MB, bytevectors 27 MB against 18 MB: +180 MB of dynamic objects that a value root holds with the cache empty. Live heap after collection: 1.367 GB against 1.034 GB. The shape (1.45 M more vectors, 170k more strings) fits realized datoms or hiccup.
(3) The index trees hold nothing realized: (:eavt db)'s _root is nil (restored by address on each read), so persistent-sorted-set's Branch _children memo (node-child) retains no tree on the live db.
Not yet known: which of root's values holds (2). compute-size over 'static reaches the whole program (952 MB for every component), so it cannot attribute. The next step is a census by boot phase (object-counts after each seon.root/boot! step) or by holder (the render cache, :results, the task index, derived problem classes), on a clone of runs/store, never on the standing root.
The fix is at the producer that pulls the whole store: it reads what its view needs, through the bounded node cache, and the cache weighs a node by its real size. Owner rule: never pull the whole db. Checked 2026-11-06: (1)'s weight and bound are no longer so: datahike.index.node-cache weighs a node by `node-bytes` (its real size, node_cache.clj:99) and `:seon.config.store/node-cache-bytes` is 134,217,728 (128 MiB); the named proof (seon.root-cost-test) is not written and test/seon has no such namespace."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.root-cost-test/a-roots-live-heap-is-flat-in-its-recorded-runs}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-idle-root-reads-nothing-from-the-store"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-10-29 (scratch root of main at 200,744 datoms, node cache under a 1 GiB bound so nothing evicts; docs/machine.md#roots-heap): after its boot reads (7,400 nodes, 136 MB weighed) an idle root's cache keeps growing, 1.6 nodes and about 200 hits a second, 0.5 MB every 20 s, with no task, message or commit arriving. Something reads main's indexes on a poll or a render with no input changed (docs/README.md: if nothing has changed, we do nothing). Find the reader (a sampled stack of the root's fibers, or the cache's misses by address and the datoms they hold), fix it at the producer so an idle root's hits and misses stay at zero. Proof: a cost proof that the cache's hits and misses over an idle interval after boot are zero."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agents-node-cache-is-sized-from-what-an-agent-reads"
  :seon.task/plan "docs/machine.md#roots-heap and :seon.config.store/node-cache-bytes: the bound is one key read by the root, the fork server and every agent, and only root's read set is measured (136 MB weighed for main at 200,744 datoms, about 0.7 KB weighed a datom, against a 128 MiB bound). The fork server reads main once for the agents it forks (seon.db/preload!) and an agent inherits that cache and adds its own reads. Measure what agents add on real tasks: an agent's node-cache-stats (hits, misses, weighed bytes) at seon_done over e2e tasks and one real task, and whether main's whole index belongs in the fork server's cache at all. Then size agents' bound from that, with a profile or a role key only if the measurement says they differ."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-held-db-is-a-reference"
  :seon.task/plan "A db value kept past its moment pins its whole version of the index (a kept db value is a reference: docs/storage.md#a-kept-db-value-is-a-reference; measured 2026-10-28: the render, vectors and reconciler procs each held ~160 MB, root's *e 1.08 GB), and today each holder works around it by hand (\"keep the commit, let the db go\" in seon.writer and seon.db/at-commit; seon.repl/walked-db and the :walk-db no longer exist in src on 2026-11-06). The owner, 2026-10-29: \"maybe we should make the db just be metadata and if you deref it you get the db ... anyone holding or getting a db is just the three metadata keys we need to retrieve it so they can go anywhere\". What exists since: `seon.db/point` (a db's [commit-id max-tx]) and `seon.db/at-commit` (the db again from a commit), which `seon.agent`'s reach graph already keys by (docs/machine.md#roots-heap); not yet one {:branch :commit :t} map with one deref. Make the reference one value: {:branch :commit :t}, plain data (it prints, crosses the wire to an agent, sits in a fact or an error), and one function in seon.db that derefs it through a cache keyed by commit (derived data: evictable, a miss is seon.db/at-commit). Measured 2026-10-29 on the bench (a 40-commit branch): making an old commit's db 0.28 ms and 412 KB, its first query 0.97 ms, the next 0.10 ms. Scope, the owner's choice of the narrow form: references wherever a db is held (process state, caches, errors, proc state, messages to agents, the walk's :walk-db), the ~950 functions and calls that take a db value within one call (426 lines in 32 files of src and src/my name :seon.host/db on 2026-11-06) left as they are. Root is an agent on main: its seon.agent/db derefs {:branch :main} (today (seon.agent/db) in a root eval throws \"no branch\"), which replaces seon.repl's agent-only condition on reading an eval's citations. Merges first as its own small task (the reference and its deref, with a cost proof); then the held sites move over in order of measured memory."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-commit-of-a-few-datoms-writes-what-it-changed"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-11-01 in a test root's agent, 30 commits of one eval record each (seon.agent/commit!, docs/machine.md#an-agents-first-write): a commit of a few datoms allocates 2.3-2.6 MB, flat over the 30 (db->stored 0.36-0.6 MB, datahike.writing/create-commit-id 0.12 MB, konserve.core/multi-assoc 1.7-1.9 MB). Each writes 21-25 values: the leaf and two to three branch levels of each index whose path it changed (current and history, :keep-history? true, v1's ruling) and three records (the commit graph entry, the commit, the branch head); the nodes' contents weigh ~200 KB (datahike.index.node-cache/node-bytes), so the write path allocates ~9x what it writes. Split on one commit's 18 nodes (1.56 MB, ~87 KB a node): konserve.impl.defaults/prepare-multi-assoc's own work 875 KB (each value serialized through a ByteArrayOutputStream, whose buffer doubles, then toByteArray, a copy; the node's write handler), konserve.jolt.zstd/compress 244 KB and konserve.jolt.fasl/encode 231 KB in 36 calls each (half of them a per-value metadata map, encoded and compressed like a node), and the LMDB write 139 KB (konserve.jolt.lmdb's concat-bytes copying header, meta and value into one array, one write transaction per key). Every eval of every agent pays it. The change, in our forks (konserve, then datahike if a part is there), each step measured before and after on the same commit: (1) split prepare-multi-assoc's 875 KB (the buffer and copy against the node handler's data) with seon.profile/counted wraps, as the measurement above did; (2) a value is serialized once into a buffer of its size (fasl's encode-bv, no ByteArrayOutputStream growth and copy); (3) a batch's uniform metadata ({:immutable? true}) encoded once and never compressed (a small map that zstd grows); (4) the LMDB backing writes a batch in one write transaction, header, meta and value put into the reserved value (MDB_RESERVE) without concat-bytes. Estimate: 2.4 MB to under 1.2 MB a commit; a measured number, not this one, goes in the commit. Done 2026-11-01 (konserve 5d89b976, jolt/serialize-once): (2) and (4)'s copy, a commit 2.3-2.6 MB -> 1.74-1.90 MB (seon.db-test/a-stored-value-is-serialized-once); (1) measured: of a batch's 1.08 MB after it, fasl's encoder (konserve.jolt.fasl, jfasl.ss kjc-encode) allocates ~20 KB a node for its ~2.6 KB of bytes (its buffer doubles from 256; every keyword's namespace and name through string->utf8 at every datom: a cache of a keyword's encoded name is the next step), zstd ~13 KB a value, freeze ~9 KB; (3) left: a value's metadata is compressed by the header's one compressor, so not compressing it changes the stored format and its reader. Not changed: what is written (the paths, the history indexes). The other lever, committing per turn rather than per eval, is the run-ahead writer's rule (seon.agent/commit!: when the agent is idle or at a turn's end) and the owner's to change. Proofs: a cost proof of one commit's bytes in seon.db (bytes and contracted calls exact at a fixed input, a commit of 1, 10 and 100 datoms: what the paths cost, never 9x), seon.agent-test/an-agents-start-costs-the-same-however-long-its-root-has-run with its :fixed lowered by what begin-run!'s first commit no longer allocates, and the forks' own gates (a pin bump runs every Jolt and fork gate: docs/merging.md#conflicts-and-pins)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-agents-start-costs-the-same-however-long-its-root-has-run seon.db-test/a-stored-value-is-serialized-once}}
 {:seon.task/name "every-attribute-has-a-real-schema"
  :seon.task/plan "The write holds each value to its attribute's schema (`seon.db/invalid-datom`, docs/storage.md#the-write-checks-every-datom), so a schema of [:map] or :any checks nothing. Checked 2026-11-06 (seon.contract/forms): :seon.model-call/error, :seon.agent/error and :seon.task/error are :seon.error/base; there is no registered key :seon.process/test-error; :seon.task/cause's :api and :evidence are :map; :seon.merge/base, ours and theirs are :seon.db/plain and :seon.merge/key is [:and {:seon.db/index true} :seon.db/plain]; :seon.db/tx-data is [:sequential :any]. Give each of those that is loose its real one; [:maybe X] under {:optional true} (absence said twice) is cleaned where met. :seon.db/tx-data tightens to ops or maps. For an attribute derived as :db.type/data the validator is [:and declared :seon.db/plain]. Then see whether `seon.contract/plainly` still has a job (seon.repl calls it, lines 639-658), and delete it if not. Measure the record-write cost proofs before and after."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "an-attribute-is-declared-because-something-uses-it"
  :seon.task/plan "Found sweeping for :ns/doc's class, an attribute declared and read that nothing wrote, 2026-11-04: seven attributes were declared and no source in src or src/my named them; :task/agent and the run's pid went with runs (2026-11-05). The three the sweep then left, :seon.doc/baseline-unread (named :seon.section/baseline-unread here), :seon.doc/dead-links and :seon.doc/reviewed, are written in seon.doc and read in seon.problems on 2026-11-06. By a text search of src for each declared attribute's keyword (seon.db/schema, 408 attributes), two outside seon.config.* are named in no source: :seon.ai.model/source and :seon.ai.model/version. No check exists and the named proof is not written. The sweep was rough: a text search of what real stores never held, read by hand. What should be true: a check says, for each declared attribute, that something writes it and something reads it; a declared attribute with neither goes, one read and never written is red. Its proof holds on today's schemas, with the seven removed or named."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-declared-attribute-is-written-and-read}}
