 {:seon.task/name "one-definition-of-each-entity"
  :seon.task/plan "STEP 1 LANDED (af7ee29464, 2026-11-08): the one transaction path (seon.db/connect, seon.db/with, seon.db/speculate; bin/laws transactions), selection by owning schema, the cascade of identity refs, membership refs optional, the findings below fixed. Left: step 2 (dissolve :seon.config/config; its entries are all optional for now), step 3 (my.test/entity generators), seon.reconcile's :work kind fallback (remove once d/db-with fixtures carry kinds), :seon.task/declaration relisting a task's attributes, and the 62 contract entries restating a registered attribute's type (34 in seon/render/ui.clj, Web's). Owner, 2026-11-08: \"All sets of keys that match schemas are validated?\"; \"get rid of the duplicate definitions for sure\"; \"Validate the ones that are there\"; \"The goal of this is to see what shakes out so don't just patch things look for deeper issues\"; \"One good definition should catch problems\". The rule (docs/data-modeling.md#a-schema-is-a-set-of-keys-written-together): an entity holding any key an entity schema requires holds them all; maps are open; every contract and map cites each key's one schema. The check (docs/storage.md#the-write-checks-every-entity-it-touches): seon.db/invalid-entity on the entities a transaction touched, refused as :seon.db/invalid-entity. Branch jolt/entity-check holds the first cut (the writer's 'transact! hook, the agent's test fork, typed refusals through both write paths, seon.db-test/a-write-holds-each-entity-it-touches-to-the-schemas-its-keys-select red without the hook).

What shook out, each to fix where the disagreement is, never by patching the test:
- Two definitions of one thing. seon.task/agent-value's contract demanded :seon.agent/branch and :seon.agent/max-turns, which a taskless agent root starts (seon.root's spawn with no task) never has, so its boot made the reconciler's observe fail on the contract: a silent stall (Web hit it by hand on root's session). Now it cites :seon.db/agent-fact and the env is built only with a branch. Same class, still open: :seon.task/declaration is a closed map relisting the task's attributes with :seon.task/kind optional while :seon.task/task-fact requires it (create-tx now writes :work; seon.reconcile still keeps its own :work default for fixtures: remove once fixtures write through the check); 62 contract entries restate a registered attribute's type (34 in seon/render/ui.clj, Web's; 12 in seon/task.clj; the rest in db, vector, repl, template, ref, turn, model).
- The giant :seon.config/config map. It does three jobs (declares the config attributes, the loader's whole-config check, the panel's order) and forced 166 keys required on any entity holding one, while a task holds single keys as overrides. Owner: dissolve it: each config key is its own schema; the loader checks each key by its own schema and refuses an unknown one; a set of config keys exists only where they must go together. Finding: neither a seon.config.* namespace nor a :seon.config/unit identifies a config key alone (177 schemas in seon.config.* namespaces, 174 in the map: :seon.config.ai/base-url and :seon.config.ai/api-key-variable are a provider entity's attributes, :seon.config.ai/reasoning-pair a value schema; :seon.ai.model/*-usd-per-mtok carry a unit too). Moving the two provider attributes to the provider's namespace makes the namespace the definition seon.program/config-key? already uses (a stored keyword rename: the standing store is erased, sessions warned).
- Schemas stricter than every writer: :seon.root/boot (a seeded store's root has none until a root boots; now optional); :schema/file (a schema registered at the REPL, my.schema/register!, has no file until a sync writes it); :seon.eval/agent (a membership ref, retracted to unlink an eval while the entity stays, seon.ctx); :form/ns and :form/order (check what a REPL definition writes).
- The check's own cost: it read each touched entity whole, so a definition, which touches its namespace's entity (a ref to every form), cost the namespace's size: seon.program-cost-test/a-definition-costs-the-form-never-the-program and seon.ctx-test/a-swap-costs-what-it-changed went over (calls 1.04x and 1.06x). Fix: presence of the required keys by one seek per (entity, key), values left to the per-datom check, no contracted helper per entity; then any bound that still moves is the owner's to approve.
- Test worlds built with d/db-with or d/with bypass the writer, so fixtures held entities production cannot write (notes with no agent, forms with only an id and source, :seon.agent/status :running outside its enum) and tests asserted on them. Owner asked whether tests exercise the check: only through a connection. Proposed: the check in Datahike's core/with (our fork), named on the db's config, so every transaction, tests included, passes it, and Seon's writer hook goes; a dependency change, the owner's go.
- The error printed its entity with pr-str beside the one printer (seon.render-test/one-printer-and-no-cut-before-it): print it through seon.print.
- :seon.db/user-fact is an [:and] of agent-fact and a map, so the first cut's entity-schemas (maps only) does not see it.

THE VETTED MODEL (field research, a writer audit and an adversarial review, 2026-11-08; owner: \"model it and make sure it's vetted\"; \"in whatever tx processing code we have we check everything rather than require functions to manually check it. Yes I want everything fixed\"):
1. One transaction path. seon.db holds the one function every transaction goes through, writes and speculations alike (the writer's batch and its 'transact!, an agent's run-ahead apply and its test fork, my.test's world, and the ~30 direct d/with, d/db-with, d/transact, dw/* calls in src today: db 4, root 7, writer 4, agent 4, fork-server 2, task, render, render.proc, reconcile, program, merge, judge, ctx, my.agent): the per-datom check (seon.db/invalid-datom, today called by hand at two sites) and the entity check, then Datahike. A law (bin/laws) names every direct Datahike transaction call outside it as red. A speculation that builds a state production cannot write is a finding, fixed at its cause (owner: \"if there's a state that we can't write that's likely a design failure\"): never exempt a call from the path.
2. Selection by ownership, one map. An entity is checked against the entity schemas that own the attributes it holds (seon.render/entity-schemas and most-specific with extends?, moved down to seon.db so the writer and the renders share it; seon.db/entity-schemas, a copy, goes; seon.ctx/identities, another, too), and holds each one's required keys, its identity included. That catches an orphan (a vector holding text but no :seon.vector/of). A started agent extends an agent (owns boot and started-ms, requires both), as a user extends an agent. Not \"any required key\" (the implicit trigger Datomic and spec2 avoid), not \"identity only\" (hides orphans).
3. Refuse only what the write broke: a required key present before and gone after, or an entity gaining an owned attribute without the rest. An entity already invalid fails no unrelated write; a scan reports it.
4. Cost: presence by one seek per (entity, required key), values left to the per-datom check, no contracted call per entity; a cost proof, flat in an entity's size and the store's.
5. Fix every disagreement at its cause. Real bugs: seon.root/release! upserts an unknown task id (a stub task); seon.root/opened! a stub agent; seon.vector leaves a vector when its subject is retracted (retractEntity retracts :seon.vector/of); seon.ctx's unlink retracts membership refs (:seon.eval/agent, :seon.cite/of, :seon.search/of, :seon.process/agent): decide each, kept-unlinked (optional) or garbage (retracted with it). Not bugs: seon.program/def-tx (same tempid, same tx); seon.source/modes-tx (latent). Schemas: :schema/file optional (REPL registration); :seon.root/boot optional (done).
6. Config dissolved: each key its own schema in a seon.config.* namespace (move :seon.config.ai/base-url, :seon.config.ai/api-key-variable, :seon.config.ai/reasoning-pair out: a store erase); every reader of the whole map moves: seon.config's keys-declared, schema-of and of-entries, invalid, the contracts taking :seon.config/config (config.clj provider, with-provider, facts; render/ui.clj config panel), seon.program's less-config-fields and the listed? bit of each config key's digest, my.schema/register! of a branch's own key, three tests.
7. Generators: my.test/entity (schema, overrides) generates the required keys plus overrides from the one schema; each ref attribute names its target schema; refs resolve from an override, an entity of that schema in the world, or one generated first; seeded from the test's name (not its digest: data must not move with unrelated edits); identities never collide (no empty strings); user-fact as a :merge, :seon.merge/entry-fact and :seon.db/plain given generators. Fixtures (~234 agent keys, 172 task ids, 89 form ids, 79 eval ids written by hand) replaced as each test is touched.
Order, each landing once proved: (1) items 1-5; (2) item 6 with the store erase; (3) item 7.
Proofs: the check's test above; a cost proof that a write's check costs its touched entities' required keys, flat in an entity's size and the store's; the law naming direct transaction calls; every refusal the full prove finds resolved at its cause."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-write-holds-each-entity-it-touches-to-the-schemas-its-keys-select}}
 {:seon.task/name "freed-addresses-are-kept-only-for-online-collection"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-09 (research for the owner's collection question): Datahike's persistent-sorted-set marks every address a commit replaces as freed (markFreed, datahike.index.persistent-set, into the store's :freed-addresses and :freed-set), for online collection to delete after a grace period (datahike.online-gc). Seon never turns online collection on, and with content addresses it must stay off (docs/storage.md#collection), so nothing drains them: 4,001 entries after 7,200 commits on a scratch store, growing for the life of a root, in every process that writes. Correct: an address is marked freed only when online collection is on (the fork's writer reads its config), so a store without it keeps no list. A change to our Datahike fork, on a jolt/ branch. Proof, red first: a store with online collection off holds no freed address after 100 commits."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merged-node-is-mains-node-whatever-the-insertion-order"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: the managing session filed it unscheduled (2026-11-10) until an agent branch lives past about 100 merges, and the owner has not chosen between the two fixes below. Filed, not scheduled (managing session, 2026-11-10): price it when an agent branch lives past about 100 merges. What exists: a node's address is its content's hash (docs/sharing.md#what-a-branchs-nodes-are), but which datoms a node holds depends on the order they were inserted: the sorted set is a plain B-tree whose full leaf of 64 is cut in half when the 65th key arrives (``org.replikativ.persistent-sorted-set/leaf-add``). seon.merge/merge-tx inserts main's datoms in another order than main's transaction did, so when a leaf overflows inside one entity's datoms the branch and main cut one or two datoms apart, and two leaves holding the same datoms between them get different addresses; they never rejoin. Measured 2026-11-10 in the scenario of seon.db-test/a-branchs-own-nodes-follow-its-own-datoms run to 100 merges: own nodes of total 3 of 157 at 20 merges, 8 of 181 at 50, 16 of 212 at 100, of which 2 to 4 hold the branch's own datoms (one :db/txInstant a merge) and the rest hold only main's datoms cut differently: about one node per 8 merges. An index on :seon.test/status moved the 20-merge count from 4 to 9 by adding one datom to the seed, which is why that test's bound is a fraction. Cost today: each such node is one leaf (up to 64 datoms) the branch stores beside main's. Two candidate fixes, neither built: (1) Datahike inserts a transaction's datoms into each index in that index's order, so a merge that carries one of main's transactions cuts as main did (n log n in the transaction's datoms; untested, and it does not help a merge that carries several transactions); (2) content-defined node boundaries in the sorted set, so a node's content is a function of the set and the class is impossible (upstream names an MST boundary the Jolt port leaves out, not read; a change to add, remove and build, a store erase, node sizes measured again). Should cost: a branch's own nodes are O(its own datoms), flat in merges. Proof when built: that test's scenario at 20 and 100 merges, own nodes equal at both."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
